We extend the two-dimensional (2D) ePlace algorithm into ePlace-3D, a flat, analytical, mixed-size placement algorithm for three-dimensional integrated circuits (3D-ICs). It applies nonlinear optimization across the entire cuboid domain. We develop:

1. eDensity-3D, an electrostatics-based 3D placement-density function with globally uniform smoothness;
2. a 3D numerical solution with an improved spectral formulation;
3. a 3D nonlinear preconditioner that speeds convergence; and
4. interleaved 2D–3D placement to improve quality and efficiency.

We combine these features in our 3D-IC placement prototype, ePlace-3D. Across all ten IBM-PLACE circuits, ePlace-3D produces 6.44% shorter wirelength and 9.11% fewer vertical interconnects (VIs) than the leading placer mPL6-3D, while running 2.55 times faster on average. Compared with NTUplace3-3D, it produces 37.15% shorter wirelength and 10.27% fewer VIs, while running 0.30 times slower on average. We also validate ePlace-3D on large-scale, modern mixed-size (MMS) 3D circuits, showing high performance and scalability.

Network communities help the URLanization and evolution of complex networks. However, developing a fast, accurate method that finds overlapping modules and partitions in a heterogeneous network has been difficult. We introduce community landscapes and ModuLand, an integrated framework that identifies overlapping network modules as hills in the community landscape and treats several widely used modularization methods as special cases. Current implementations of the community landscape concept quickly analyze weighted and directed networks. They:

1. identify overlapping modules at high resolution;
2. reveal hierarchical network structures in unprecedented detail, allowing quick, close-up analysis of large networks;
3. identify key network elements; and
4. help predict network dynamics.

The concept creates many opportunities for new methods and applications, including network routing, classification, comparison, and prediction.
